  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Evaluating Square Roots of Complex Numbers in Rectangular and Polar Form</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text>&lt;p&gt;Evaluate \( \displaystyle \sqrt{z}\) given that \(z={@z_real@} + {@z_imag@}i\).&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Note:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;ol&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;&lt;p style="color:blue"&gt;Give all the roots!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;&lt;p style="color:blue"&gt;You might want to write \(z\) in polar form first, with your solution in \(\pi\) radians, or in the form \(\displaystyle z=re^{i\theta}\) to simplify the calculations.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;!-- This is commented out 
&lt;p&gt;\(r =\) &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;\(\theta=\)  (in \(\pi\) radians&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;\(z =\) (in polar form)&lt;/p&gt; --&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Possible values for \(\sqrt{z}:\)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;\(\sqrt{z}= \) [[input:ans4]] or \(\sqrt{z}= \) [[input:ans5]] &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;\(\quad\) [[validation:ans4]] \(\quad \) [[validation:ans5]]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;[[feedback:prt4]]&lt;/p&gt;</text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text>&lt;h3&gt;&lt;b&gt;Worked solution&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Given \( \displaystyle z={@z_real@} + {@z_imag@}i\), let's express \(z\) in polar form:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Magnitude (\(r\)) of \(z\):&lt;/p&gt;
    \(\displaystyle r = \sqrt{({@z_real@} )^2 + ({@z_imag@})^2} = \sqrt{{@z_real^2@} + {@z_imag^2@}} = \sqrt{{@z_real^2+ z_imag^2@}} = {@ta1@} \)&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Argument (\(\theta\)) of \(z\):&lt;/p&gt;
    \( \displaystyle \theta = \arctan\left(\frac{\text{Im}(z)}{\text{Re}(z)}\right) = \arctan\left(\frac{{@z_imag@}}{{@z_real@}}\right) \)
    \(\displaystyle  \theta= {@arg@} \approx {@decimalplaces(arg,2)@}\)&lt;/li&gt;

 &lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Now, \(z\) in polar form becomes:&lt;/p&gt;
\(\displaystyle  z = re^{i\theta} = {@radius@}e^{{@arg@}i} ={@ta3@}\)

&lt;/li&gt;
 &lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Expressing the square root of \(z\) in polar form:&lt;/p&gt;
\(\displaystyle  \sqrt{z} = \sqrt{ {@radius@}}e^{{@arg@}i}=\sqrt{{@ta3@}} \)
 &lt;p&gt;Now, for possible values of  \(\displaystyle  \sqrt{z} ,\) we consider the fact that the square root function introduces a \( \pm \) sign:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;\(\displaystyle  \sqrt{z} = {@ta4@} \) or &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;\(\displaystyle \sqrt{z} = {@ta5@} \)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;</text>
    </generalfeedback>
